Switchyard patch 0005 (app/scripts/patch-exact.sh applies it, after 0001-0004): macOS host only.
After a batch that only gives text new words, the Mac presenter's text pass asks only of the
paragraphs that batch touched, instead of every paragraph near the viewport.

Problem. Presenter.batchApplied (host/apple/Sources/ExactKit/Mac/PresenterMac.swift) ran
refreshVisibleText(limit: 0) after every batch, and every batch with ops dropped the
TextViewportIndex. So a running timer's text changing once a second rebuilt the index (a
convertRect per shown paragraph, the whole page) and walked every paragraph within 1600 pt of
the viewport. Per paragraph the walk asks canRasterText three times and textIsSmall once (four
pixelScale -> convertToBacking transform walks and three canvasAbove ancestor walks), even
though for a small label (most UI text) every answer is "nothing to do". Measured on an M-series
Mac mini (Switchyard 0.1.8 candidate, Live tab idle with three running timers, `sample` 10 s):
refreshVisibleText was 23 of 77 non-waiting samples of the whole process (30 %; canRasterText
16, pixelScale 9, TextViewportIndex.init 5, replaceVisible 5, canvasAbove 4), the largest single
cost of a ~1.85 % CPU idle.

Design. A batch is "words only" when every op is a paragraph's runs, a props op that sets or
clears only `text`, `title` or `accessibilityLabel`, or an svg/animations/canvas2d/surface/
surfaceWork op (wordsOnly). Such a batch keeps the index and records the ids it touched, with
each id's paragraph owner and text ancestors, as `touched` walks them (textTouched; any other
batch, nested ones included, sets it to nil and drops the index as before). After the outermost
batch, batchApplied runs the same refreshVisibleText(limit: 0), with candidates filtered to the
touched ids and every other band entry kept, when textQuiet holds; otherwise it drops the index
(when the batches had ops, as before) and runs the full pass. textQuiet:
- no text work is owed: textPending is false, so the last pass settled every other paragraph
  (anything left unsettled sets textPending and the pump's full passes own it);
- not inside a scroll callback, no capture, no selection, no flight, no exit, no reader paragraph;
- TextViewportIndex.unmoved: no NodeView moved, resized, mounted, unmounted or reparented, and no
  BlockView (a moved subtree's container) was placed, since the index was built
  (NodeView.textGeometry, as iOS's TextIndexIOS.swift); and each indexed document's visibleRect
  and convertToBacking(1x1) are as they were (no scroll at any level, window resize, page zoom
  or display/backing change).
Scroll callbacks, the pump, settlePump and topLayerChanged still run the full pass.

Why the output is the same. The full pass after a words-only batch differs from the restricted
one only in untouched paragraphs. For those, every input of the pass is unchanged since the last
pass that settled them: their geometry and candidate set (unmoved), their raster keys (spec,
size, box and pixel scale: no op reached them, unmoved), their bands (unmoved), selection and
capture state, and the raster workers' outstanding work (textPending false). So the full pass
would do nothing for them: no replaceVisible job, no ensure, no admit. Text work arising between
batches never relied on a later batch (an idle page has none): it is driven by the display path
(updateLayer/draw -> ensure, requestTextPublication), scroll callbacks and the pump. The only
state the restricted pass leaves that the full pass would not: visibleText entries that draw()
added lazily for paragraphs that do not paint through bands; they are pruned by the next full
pass and are not read meanwhile. Touched paragraphs get exactly the old per-node treatment
(same order, same limit, same replaceVisible batching and wait).

Verified (Switchyard sy/mac-p7, app/PERF.md "Pass 7"): Exact host XCTests of the text, scroll,
zoom and split paths on macOS; macOS screenshots base vs head at the same agent-clock moments
after timer ticks, a scroll of a long list, a resize and new rows: pixel-identical; `sample` and
`ps` CPU of Live idle with timers, base vs head, interleaved on the mini. The web is untouched.

